// Source for /img/render_rates.png, embedded by
// docs/developer_guides/dataflow.md under "Where the GPU sits".
//
// ONE CHAIN, THREE DOORS INTO IT, and the vertical position of a door is the
// whole content: where a change enters IS what it costs. The section under this
// figure states the rule in prose -- what `rpcProps()` returns refetches, what
// `gpuProps()` returns re-uploads, everything else is a frame -- and a rule
// with three cases and an ordering is the thing a picture does better than a
// sentence, because the ordering is spatial.
//
// It exists BESIDE two figures that draw pieces of the same path, and the split
// is by what each answers:
//
//   dataflow.dot             the whole path, one track, one region, with the
//                            worker's internals and the caches on it. Its
//                            upload edge names both of its triggers, which is
//                            as far as a path figure can carry this: it draws
//                            what runs, not what a change costs.
//   gpu_display_lifecycle    the same three doors on the REACTIVE graph --
//     .dot                   autoruns, renderTick, canvasDrawn -- for someone
//                            writing a display. The pair is the one
//                            gpu_display_tldr.dot and that figure already are:
//                            same architecture, two zooms.
//
// So the chain here is COLLAPSED to the six steps a door can land between, and
// carries no autorun, no tick and no HAL. `encode on the main thread` is on it
// for one reason: it is the only stage the middle door can enter, and every
// figure that leaves it out has to draw a re-upload as either a refetch or a
// frame, which is the error the page under this used to make.
//
// The door labels are the same strings gpu_display_lifecycle.dot uses, in the
// same order and the same light blue. A reader meeting both should recognise
// three doors they have seen, not meet a second taxonomy.
//
// `theme` is deliberately NOT in the frame door's list, though it looks like it
// belongs: a theme change moves palette-derived colors, and for a display that
// packs its colors that is the middle door, not this one. Opacity is the
// example that is safe, and it is safe for a reason ARCHITECTURE.md spends a
// section on ("opacity is a render parameter, never a packed color").
digraph render_rates {
  rankdir=TB
  labelloc=t
  fontname="Helvetica"
  fontsize=18
  label="Refetch, re-upload, redraw: where a change enters the chain"
  nodesep=0.3
  ranksep=0.45

  node [shape=box style="filled,rounded" fillcolor="#ffffff" color="#9e9e9e"
        fontname="Helvetica" fontsize=12 margin="0.18,0.10"]
  edge [color="#555555" fontname="Helvetica" fontsize=10]

  // ── The chain, one column ─────────────────────────────────────────
  // Colored by what each step is, on dataflow.dot's legend: blue for the
  // worker, yellow for what survives the next gesture, orange for the GPU.
  worker [label="worker: fetch, parse, pack" fillcolor="#e3f2fd" color="#90caf9"]
  data [label="rpcDataMap" fillcolor="#fff9c4" color="#ffe082"]
  encode [label="encode on the main thread\nlayout, colors, packed instances" fillcolor="#f1f8e9" color="#aed581"]
  upload [label="backend.upload()" fillcolor="#ffe0b2" color="#ffcc80"]
  draw [label="backend.renderBlocks()" fillcolor="#ffe0b2" color="#ffcc80"]
  screen [label="<canvas>" fillcolor="#e1f5fe" color="#90caf9"]

  worker -> data [label="  transferables, uint32 bp  "]
  data -> encode
  encode -> upload
  upload -> draw [label="  renderTick++  "]
  draw -> screen

  // ── The three doors ───────────────────────────────────────────────
  // A shared width, so the three read as one column of doors rather than as
  // three boxes sized by how long their text happens to be, and each is on the
  // rank of the step it enters.
  node [width=3.9 style="filled,rounded,dashed" fillcolor="#e1f5fe" color="#90caf9"]
  d1 [label="a region with no loaded data,\nor rpcProps() changed"]
  d2 [label="gpuProps() changed,\nor a derived region map"]
  d3 [label="scroll, zoom, opacity, resize"]

  d1 -> worker [label=<  <b>refetch</b>  >]
  d2 -> encode [label=<  <b>re-encode, re-upload</b><br align="left"/>  no fetch<br align="left"/>>]
  d3 -> draw [label=<  <b>redraw</b><br align="left"/>  no fetch, no upload<br align="left"/>>]

  // Safe as ranksets because no member is inside a cluster — there are none
  // here. Each pairs a door with the step it enters, which is what makes the
  // depth of a door mean something.
  { rank=same; d1; worker }
  { rank=same; d2; encode }
  { rank=same; d3; draw }
}
